  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "h1",
      "text": "Do Braces Hurt? How Long the Pain Lasts and How to Cope"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Home Blog 13th Aug, 2025",
      "links": [
        {
          "href": "https://drdantegonzales.com/index.php",
          "text": "Home"
        },
        {
          "href": "https://drdantegonzales.com/blog.php",
          "text": "Blog"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Ever wondered, do teeth braces hurt as much as people say? You’re not alone. Getting braces is a big step for your smile, but it also raises a lot of questions. Pain tops the list. Will it hurt all the time? How bad is the pain really? How long does it last? The truth is, yes, braces do hurt at times."
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "But the discomfort is usually temporary and manageable. And most importantly, it means your treatment is working towards braces pain relief. The pain tells you that your teeth are shifting into place. Let’s break it all down and explore how to handle braces pain smartly."
    },
    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "Do Braces Hurt? What to Expect"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Yes, braces do hurt, especially during the initial days and after adjustments. But the pain is not unbearable. It feels more like pressure on your teeth. That’s because braces apply gentle force to move teeth into new positions."
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "You may feel slight pain, soreness, or mild discomfort in your gums, jaw, and even cheeks. You may also experience soreness from small sores or irritation from wires and brackets. But don't worry, it’s completely normal and fades with time. With a few tricks and proper care, pain relief is easier than you think."
    },
    {
      "type": "img",
      "src": "../assets/blog/shared/orthodontic-illustration-01.jpg",
      "alt": "",
      "title": null
    },
    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "When Does the Pain Occur?"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Brace pain usually comes in phases. Understanding when it happens helps you stay prepared for a beautiful smile. Most people experience discomfort in the following situations:"
    },
    {
      "type": "h3",
      "text": "1. Right After Getting Braces"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "You’ll feel the most initial discomfort in the first 4 to 7 days. Your teeth feel tight, your gums may feel mild discomfort and sore, and even chewing soft foods may be a task. This pain means the treatment has started."
    },
    {
      "type": "h3",
      "text": "2. After Every Adjustment"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Each time your orthodontist tightens the wires, expect slight pain or pressure for a few hours or a couple of days. This is again normal for patients undergoing orthodontic treatment. It’s a sign that your teeth are moving properly."
    },
    {
      "type": "h3",
      "text": "3. When You Start Wearing Rubber Bands"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Rubber bands add more force to specific areas. So yes, they may hurt when you begin using them. But once your mouth adjusts, the pain reduces significantly."
    },
    {
      "type": "h3",
      "text": "4. During Wire or Bracket Breakage"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "If a bracket breaks or a wire pokes your cheeks or gums, expect soreness. This kind of discomfort is different from the normal pressure pain and may be relieved by oral anesthetics."
    },
    {
      "type": "h3",
      "text": "5. When Small Sores Develop"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Sometimes, small cuts or sore areas may develop on your inner cheeks or tongue. Braces rubbing against soft tissues is the main reason. These sores are uncomfortable but usually go away."
    },
    {
      "type": "h3",
      "text": "6. When You Don’t Follow Instructions"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Ignoring your orthodontist’s advice, like skipping rubber bands or eating acidic foods, can lead to extra pain. Misalignment or added pressure causes unnecessary discomfort."
    },
    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "Common Types of Discomfort With Braces"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Let’s break down the types of pain or discomfort people often feel and how to relieve them. It’s not just one kind. Braces can trigger various sensations, depending on where and how they apply pressure."
    },
    {
      "type": "h3",
      "text": "1. Tooth Pain and Pressure"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "This is the most common kind. Braces put pressure on your teeth to shift them. The resulting ache is a natural response, and it lessens once your teeth settle into their new spots."
    },
    {
      "type": "h3",
      "text": "2. Gum Inflammation"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "When your gums are shifting, they may swell or feel puffy. Reduce inflammation by rinsing with salt water or using an ice pack. It's part of your mouth adjusting to the pressure."
    },
    {
      "type": "h3",
      "text": "3. Sore Cheeks and Tongue"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Brackets and wires often rub against the inner cheek or tongue. This can cause small sores or irritation. Use orthodontic wax and avoid acidic or spicy foods to let it heal."
    },
    {
      "type": "h3",
      "text": "4. Jaw Soreness"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Sometimes your jaw feels stiff, especially after adjustments. It may be hard to chew. Stick to soft foods like mashed potatoes or khichdi during this time."
    },
    {
      "type": "h3",
      "text": "5. Headaches"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Some patients report mild headaches when the pressure shifts to the jaw. Proper hydration, rest, and over-the-counter pain relievers help manage it."
    },
    {
      "type": "h3",
      "text": "6. Broken or Loose Brackets"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "A broken bracket or a wire poking your cheek can cause sharp pain. It’s important to visit your orthodontist quickly. Meanwhile, use wax or a cotton swab to protect the sore area."
    },
    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "How Long Does Braces Pain Last?"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Here’s the good news: braces hurt only for a short while. The worst pain is usually within the first 5 to 7 days of getting your new braces. After that, the mouth adjusts, and you feel more normal. Each adjustment may bring back some soreness, but it fades in 1 to 3 days."
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "With every passing month, your mouth becomes stronger and more tolerant of the pressure. The entire process becomes less painful as you go. So yes, there’s pain, but it doesn’t last forever."
    },
    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "Pain Relief Options for Braces"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Pain is part of the process, but you don’t have to suffer. Here are the best home remedies for relieving braces pain safely."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "1. Over-the-Counter Pain Relievers: Medicines like Advil, ibuprofen, or paracetamol help reduce pain or discomfort. Take them as your doctor recommends, especially after new braces or adjustments."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "2. Use Ice Packs: Apply an ice pack to your jaw or cheeks. Cold therapy helps reduce inflammation and numbs the area. Wrap ice in a towel and use it for 15 minutes at a time."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "3. Eat Soft Foods: Stick to a soft foods diet during painful days. Try to eat soft foods like curd rice, dalia, khichdi, bananas, or mashed potatoes. Avoid crunchy, hard, or sticky items that apply pressure."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "4. Use Orthodontic Wax: When brackets rub against your cheeks or tongue, apply orthodontic wax. It creates a cushion and prevents irritation. It’s safe to use daily if needed."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "5. Oral Anesthetics: Numbing gels like benzocaine can be applied using a cotton swab on sore areas. They offer quick pain relief when applied directly."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "6. Salt Water Rinse: A warm rinse made with salt and water helps heal small cuts and sore areas. Do this twice a day, especially before bedtime."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "7. Avoid Acidic Foods: Acidic foods like lemon, tomato, or soda can worsen mouth sores. They cause stinging and slow healing. Stick to bland or cooling foods when your mouth is sore."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "8. Stay Hydrated: Dry mouth can increase irritation. Drink plenty of water. It keeps tissues healthy and helps with overall oral health."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "9. Avoid Chewing on the Sore Side: If one side of your mouth hurts more, chew on the other side for a few days. Let the sore side heal without added pressure."
    },
    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "Tips to Manage and Reduce Braces Discomfort"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "You can stay ahead of the pain with a few smart daily habits. These tips make a big difference in your entire process."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "1. Follow a Routine Clean your braces, rinse regularly, and follow food guidelines."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "2. Keep Wax Handy Carry orthodontic wax wherever you go. Apply it at the first sign of rubbing."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "3. Use Cold Water Sipping cold water soothes sore gums, especially after tightening."
    },
    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "When Should You See an Orthodontist for Braces Pain?"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Some pain is normal. But constant or intense pain isn’t, and you should seek ways to stop the pain immediately. See your orthodontist if:"
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Pain lasts more than a week."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Wires are poking, or brackets are broken."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "You see swelling or pus in the gums."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "You can't eat or sleep because of the pain."
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Delaying treatment can cause more problems. Your orthodontist will fix the issue and suggest the best pain relief method."
    },
    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "Conclusion"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Your journey to a perfect smile doesn’t have to be painful. While braces hurt at times, with the right support and guidance, you can manage them smoothly. At Dante Gonzales Orthodontics, we understand your concerns and offer compassionate, customized orthodontic treatment. From day one, we help you feel comfortable and confident."
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "If you’re getting your new braces, dealing with initial discomfort, or worried about rubber bands, we’re here to support you. Book your appointment today and explore our pain-free, expert solutions. Don’t wait for the pain to get worse. Get started with a consultation that puts your smile first."
    },
